issue:
Bug: no-use-before-define does not treat IIFE bodies as the same execution context

### Environment

Node version: v22.22.3
npm version: v10.9.8
Local ESLint version: v10.8.1
Global ESLint version: not installed
Operating System: macOS 26.1

### What parser are you using?

Default (Espree)

### What did you do?

<details><summary>Configuration</summary>

```js
export default [
    {
        rules: {
            "no-use-before-define": ["error", { "variables": false }]
        }
    }
];
```

</details>

```js
/*eslint no-use-before-define: ["error", { "variables": false }]*/

(() => {
    console.log(a);
})();

const a = 5;
```

### What did you expect to happen?

An error on `a`, because the IIFE body runs immediately — before `a` is initialized — so this throws `ReferenceError: Cannot access 'a' before initialization` at runtime.

The rule already handles the analogous case for class static initializer blocks, which are also provably executed immediately. That case is listed as **incorrect** code for `{ "variables": false }` in the [rule docs](https://eslint.org/docs/latest/rules/no-use-before-define#variables):

```js
/*eslint no-use-before-define: ["error", { "variables": false }]*/

{
    class C {
        static {
            this.x = foo; // reported
        }
    }
    const foo = 1;
}
```

I'd expect an IIFE body to be treated the same way, since its immediacy is equally provable at the call site.

### What actually happened?

No error is reported. Verified on ESLint v10.8.1:

```
$ npx eslint test.js
(no output)

$ node test.js
ReferenceError: Cannot access 'a' before initialization
```

This affects both `{ "variables": false }` and `{ "classes": false }`, and every IIFE shape I tried:

```js
/*eslint no-use-before-define: ["error", { "variables": false }]*/

// 1. arrow IIFE — not reported
(() => { console.log(a); })();

// 2. function expression IIFE — not reported
(function () { console.log(a); })();

// 3. awaited async IIFE — not reported
await (async () => { console.log(a); })();

// 4. nested IIFEs — not reported
(() => { (() => { console.log(a); })(); })();

const a = 5;
```

```js
/*eslint no-use-before-define: ["error", { "classes": false }]*/

// 5. class reference inside an IIFE — not reported
(() => { new C(); })();

class C {}
```

For contrast, these are correctly **not** reported today and must stay that way:

```js
/*eslint no-use-before-define: ["error", { "variables": false }]*/

// deferred — the function isn't called until after `a` is initialized
const f = () => { console.log(a); };
const a = 5;
f();
```

```js
/*eslint no-use-before-define: ["error", { "variables": false }]*/

// the IIFE runs immediately, but the returned inner function is deferred
(() => () => a)();
const a = 5;
```

Note that with the default options (`variables: true`) case 1 *is* reported, so this only affects configurations that opt out via `variables: false` / `classes: false`.

### Link to Minimal Reproducible Example

https://eslint.org/play/#

### Participation

- [X] I am willing to submit a pull request for this issue.

### AI acknowledgment

- [ ] I did not use AI to generate this issue report.
- [ ] (If the above is not checked) I have reviewed the AI-generated content before submitting.

### Additional comments

This is the follow-up issue that @mdjermanovic asked for in #20014, so that IIFE handling can be tracked separately from the initializer question discussed there:

> I think treating code in IIFEs as code that runs in the upper execution context is not directly related to https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/2001
